xieties about the loss of individuality and the consequences of an unbridled scientific progress. Huxley's narrative is set against the backdrop of early 20th-century global upheaval, including the aftermath of World War I and the rise of consumer culture, making it a critical commentary on the contemporary socio-political landscape.

